{
  "sourceId": "2016-P1-Q16",
  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125112/TMUA-2016-paper-1.pdf#page=11\">TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 16</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07125113/TMUA-2016-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=19\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 16",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2016 · Paper 1 · Question 16 — Determine both y-values satisfying a pair of logarithmic equations.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "State the logarithm domains",
      "body": "<p>The equations require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Combine the first pair of logarithms and use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in the second equation.</p>",
      "recap": "The algebraic equations are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "pitfall": "A logarithm equal to zero has argument 1, not 0.",
      "pause": "Express <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in terms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l06",
          "reminder": "A logarithm equation must be converted using its definition and positive argument."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Eliminate the unwanted variable",
      "body": "<p>The second equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Substituting into the first gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The resulting quadratic is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "pitfall": "Square the full numerator <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Complete the square or use the quadratic formula.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "Keep all terms when substituting a linear expression into a square."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check both candidate values",
      "body": "<p>For each root of the quadratic, check that the first logarithm argument is positive and use the linear equation to recover the other variable. Check the argument of the second logarithm as well.</p>",
      "recap": "A root of a rearranged equation is a candidate until every original domain condition is checked.",
      "pitfall": "Do not reject a surd branch without testing its actual sign and domain.",
      "pause": "Calculate both candidates and test all three logarithm arguments before selecting the matching option.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l02",
          "reminder": "A solution of a transformed equation must also satisfy the original definitions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Logarithm laws give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and the second equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>18</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. Both are greater than 1 because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, and their associated x-values are positive. The second logarithm’s argument equals 1, so both solutions are valid.</p><p><strong>Answer C: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</strong></p>",
